{"name":"Sail Maker","hex":"#0e4d72","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sail","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A piece of fabric attached to a boat and arranged such that it causes the wind to drive the boat along. The sail may be attached to the boat via a combination of mast, spars and ropes.","The concept of a sail or sails, as if a substance."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To be impelled or driven forward by the action of wind upon sails, as a ship on water; to be impelled on a body of water by steam or other power.","To move through or on the water; to swim, as a fish or a waterfowl."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sail"},{"term":"maker","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Someone who makes; a person or thing that makes or produces something.","God, the Creator."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/maker"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sailmaker","blocks":[{"text":"A sailmaker makes and repairs sails for sailboats, kites, hang gliders, wind art, architectural sails, or other structures using sails. A sailmaker typically works on shore in a sail loft; the sail loft has other sailmakers. Large ocean-going sailing ships often had sailmakers in the crew, maintaining and repairing sails. This required knowledge of the sailmaker's craft and the tools of the sailmakers loft on shore."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/The_Women%27s_Royal_Naval_Service_on_the_Home_Front%2C_1917-1918_Q19759.jpg/330px-The_Women%27s_Royal_Naval_Service_on_the_Home_Front%2C_1917-1918_Q19759.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sailmaker","wikidata":"Q549237"},{"title":"Sail","blocks":[{"text":"A sail is a tensile structure, which is made from fabric or other membrane materials, that uses wind power to propel sailing craft, including sailing ships, sailboats, windsurfers, ice boats, and even sail-powered land vehicles. Sails may be made from a combination of woven materials—including canvas or polyester cloth, laminated membranes or bonded filaments, usually in a three- or four-sided shape."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Chesapeake.jpg/330px-Chesapeake.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sail","wikidata":"Q25999"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/USS_Monongahela_%281862%29.jpg/500px-USS_Monongahela_%281862%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/USS_Monongahela_%281862%29.jpg/500px-USS_Monongahela_%281862%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The USS Monongahela (1862), a vessel exemplifying the 19th-century sailmakers' craft","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUSS_Monongahela_(1862).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Sail_Loft_Photo_Don_Ramey_Logan.jpg/500px-Sail_Loft_Photo_Don_Ramey_Logan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Sail_Loft_Photo_Don_Ramey_Logan.jpg/1280px-Sail_Loft_Photo_Don_Ramey_Logan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Sail_Loft_Photo_Don_Ramey_Logan.jpg/500px-Sail_Loft_Photo_Don_Ramey_Logan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sewing machine for sails","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASail_Loft_Photo_Don_Ramey_Logan.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Sailmaker%27s_stamp.jpg/500px-Sailmaker%27s_stamp.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikiped